                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A cute game with an interesting premise, but *man* is it tiring and boring. I get what they were going for with the whole \"heal the knight who does the damage\" angle, but i did more micro-managing of the knight than I did the healer. IN fact, most of my mana was buffing EXP rates and buffing the knight's damage rather than actually healing. Enemy balance is all over the place - scarecrows just delete a limb for fun, cockatrices do similar (but that at least can be healed) and freezing and burning are insanely dangerous but completely unstoppable. The Game will absolutely and happily plonk three mice witches and have two of them charging their full-body freezing laser simultaneously, and there is almost no way for you to be able to deal with that.\r\n\r\nOn top of that, I think I had to manage torches more than healing. Seriously, what possible fun and interaction does the torch system offer? It's nothing to do with healing, it's not engaging, and until you dump a ton of upgrades into it the whole \"recycle items into more time\" thing is an absolutely terrible investment. Not to mention that there's an entire game mode dedicated to ignoring those upgrades, and another mode that will just drop you into an endgame room on the first level. \r\n\r\nThe concept is really neat, and I really enjoy the idea of it - but man, the actual execution and reality of playing the game is just really off base. In a game about being a healer, I want to actually be healing instead of buffing XP rates and refilling torches. Directing the knight isn't so bad, but the game ends up being \"mindlessly mash the same few buttons until the RNG doesn't screw you over\", and it's just... boring  and uninteresting. Really cool concept and solid design, but really dissapointing by the end.",
